# Who owns what in this repository, and a record of what that ownership does
# and does not cause. Ported from the Terraform repository this one
# reproduces, with its measurements condensed rather than re-run: the
# platform behaviour they describe is GitHub's, not that repository's.
#
# It is not a merge gate and does not become one by existing. "Require review
# from Code Owners" stays off in the `protect-main` ruleset — created after
# the first pull request, with the seven required contexts validate.yml
# reports — and docs/BOOTSTRAP.md section 7.2, written in Phase 5, holds the
# argument for why that setting and the required approving review beside it
# both stay off on a repository with one maintainer. That argument is stated
# once, there, and is not repeated here.
#
# Placement is load-bearing. GitHub reads `.github/`, then the repository
# root, then `docs/`, and uses the first of those it finds. This file is in
# `.github/`, which is both first in that order and the location GitHub's own
# hardening guidance names. The part worth writing down is that this
# repository has a `docs/` directory: a `docs/CODEOWNERS` added later would be
# shadowed by this one and would be silently dead, with no error raised
# anywhere. There is none today.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this file never does: request a review from the maintainer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GitHub does not request a review from the author of a pull request, even
# when that author is the code owner, and its documentation does not say so
# — the Terraform repository measured it on 2026-08-31 against 800 pull
# requests across eight large public repositories, zero self-requests, with
# two repositories that name individual users showing the control
# symmetrically: owner A's pull requests request only owner B, owner B's only
# owner A, and neither is ever requested on its own. The whole model is three
# lines: a non-draft pull request requests every owner except its author, a
# draft requests nobody, and the author is never requested. On a repository
# whose every pull request is opened by the single account named below, that
# is most of what there is to say about this file's day-to-day effect.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it does operate: Dependabot's pull requests
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The reading that follows from the section above is that this file is inert
# until a second maintainer arrives. That reading is wrong, and what makes it
# wrong is already committed: .github/dependabot.yml proposes GitHub Actions
# and Python dependency bumps weekly on Tuesdays.
#
# A Dependabot pull request is authored by `dependabot[bot]`, which is not the
# owner below, so the rule that exempts an author does not reach it. Measured
# in the same two control repositories — neither of whose Dependabot
# configurations sets `reviewers:` or `assignees:` — every
# `dependabot[bot]`-authored pull request examined requested review from every
# code owner.
#
# So the accurate statement is neither "it drives review requests" nor "it
# does nothing until there is a second maintainer". It drives requests on pull
# requests opened by somebody other than the owner, which on this repository
# means Dependabot's and any outside contributor's.
#
# That weekly request is kept rather than suppressed, and it could be
# suppressed: an empty owner list is legal and removes ownership for the
# pattern it follows, so a `.github/workflows/` line with nothing after it
# would silence exactly those requests and leave the rest of the tree owned.
# What that costs is the file. `.github/workflows/` is where the apply roles
# are assumed, and `pyproject.toml` with `uv.lock` is where the provider is
# pinned; those are the parts of the tree a Dependabot pull request touches,
# and disowning them to avoid one notification a week would leave a CODEOWNERS
# whose every remaining effect is one of the passive ones below — the "off
# switch with extra steps" shape section 7.2 turns down. The request is also
# the only recurring evidence that the handle below still resolves: a signal
# somebody has to be watching for rather than a check, but one that costs
# nothing to keep.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it does on the pull requests where it requests nobody
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Four things, none of them a gate, and together they are why this file is
# worth having on a repository where it will request review from nobody on
# almost every pull request:
#
#   - file and directory views on github.com carry an ownership shield with
#     the owner in its tooltip, which answers "who do I ask about this" in
#     the place the question gets asked;
#   - a pull request's compare view previews which code owners the pull
#     request will request, before it is opened;
#   - syntax errors in this file are highlighted inline when it is viewed on
#     github.com, and are machine-readable at
#     `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/codeowners/errors` — see the block below;
#   - it arms `require_code_owner_review`, which is the setting section 7.2
#     turns on the day the argument for leaving it off stops holding. Without
#     a CODEOWNERS that setting has nothing to enforce.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The owner
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One pattern, and it is the whole repository. `*` matches every file at every
# depth, so ownership is total and there is no unowned corner for a later
# pattern to have to remember. Order matters in a CODEOWNERS — the last
# matching pattern wins, not the most specific one — which is a rule with
# nothing to trip over while there is one line.
#
# The handle is the real account this repository is developed in rather than a
# placeholder, for the reason the committed stack files will write out their
# `namePrefix` from Phase 1: a `<owner>` written here would be a file that
# resolves to nobody, does nothing, and reports no error while doing it. A
# GitHub handle is not among the things this repository launders — the
# Terraform repository's docs/BOOTSTRAP.md scopes that to account ids, ARNs
# and bucket names, all of which differ per cloner and one of which is the
# likeliest leak in the project, and this repository's runbook keeps the
# scope — and `isai2105` is already written out in .github/dependabot.yml,
# and from Phase 1 on in bootstrap/Pulumi.bootstrap.yaml and both
# environments' stack files. A cloner replaces this line and nothing else.
#
# Nothing in this tree can be read to tell whether the handle on the right
# resolves — only GitHub holds that — and the failure is silent by
# construction: GitHub skips a line naming a user who does not exist or lacks
# write access, assigns no owner, and raises no error, no annotation and no
# check run. That is the shape of rule 2 in .github/workflows/validate.yml,
# an obligation held by nobody, and it is not left that way here. The
# `audit-online` job in that file reads `codeowners/errors` at the pull
# request's own ref on every pull request. It catches a handle that stopped
# resolving, and it catches a cloner's typo on their first pull request rather
# than months later.
#
# What that check does not cover is measured rather than assumed. The endpoint
# reports unknown owners and misses invalid *patterns* entirely — reproduced
# in the Terraform repository against a file whose `!negated` line the web UI
# flags as an invalid pattern and the API does not mention. With one pattern,
# and that pattern `*`, there is nothing here for it to miss today. The commit
# that adds a second pattern is the commit that makes the gap real.
#
# The check can also decline to answer. That endpoint caches per exact ref
# string and serves a sticky 404 for a string first queried before its index
# was built. A run that hits one of those warns instead of checking, and
# .github/workflows/validate.yml holds the three-way test that tells it apart
# from a CODEOWNERS that really is gone.
* @isai2105

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decisions not taken
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Per-path ownership lines are deliberately absent. `bootstrap/`, `envs/`,
# `packages/static-site/`, `policy/`, `docs/` and `.github/workflows/` are
# real seams, and a line each naming the same owner would be functionally
# identical to the single `*` above under last-match-wins: the same output,
# six times the file. The case for writing them anyway is anticipatory — they
# mark where a future split goes, so handing an area over becomes a one-word
# edit.
#
# They are turned down on this repository's own rule, which
# .github/dependabot.yml states against `open-pull-requests-limit` and
# `prefix-development`: a line that does nothing today is a line that can only
# be wrong. Six identical owners encode a distinction that does not exist and
# read, to anyone scanning the file, as though they do something. They are
# also unverifiable in a way `*` is not — a path that is renamed or removed
# leaves a pattern that matches nothing, which is not a syntax error and so is
# reported by neither the web UI nor `codeowners/errors`. The split, when it
# comes, is derived against the tree as it is then rather than against this
# one, and re-deriving six lines is not the expensive part of it.
#
# A `@team` handle is not an option declined here so much as one that does not
# exist. Team syntax is `@org/team-name`, teams exist only inside
# organizations, and this repository is owned by a `User`. The neighbouring
# constraint is worth writing down because it is invisible until it bites:
# code owners are a feature of public repositories on GitHub Free, so making
# this repository private would stop this file being read — no error, no
# annotation, and every effect listed above gone at once.
#
# `require_code_owner_review` in the `protect-main` ruleset stays `false`
# when that ruleset is created, and the argument for that is docs/BOOTSTRAP.md
# section 7.2 rather than these lines, because it is the same argument that
# keeps a required approving review off and the repository states a shared
# argument once. Section 7.2 also carries the payload that turns both on the
# day a second maintainer exists.
